From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: ECON: Utility of Medical Economics

There seem to have been two forms of reply to my post about medical spending:

1) The amount of medical spending will go up if a Redistrbutionist State
is liberalized;

2) Who cares?  The Redistributionists will do as they please since they're
in power anyways, so all this is irrelevant.

Let me remind you that I was replying to a claim of Dale Worley's that a
free market in medical charity would have to spend as much as the State
currently does.  My attack on this seems to have succeeded, regardless of
either 1 or 2.

Responding to the latter first, many labor under the misconception that
State Medicine is a rational response to market failure.  It is to them
that my point was addressed, not to the First Lady.

As for the first, perhaps I was overbroad.  I was speaking of the short-run
for medical charity, not the long run for all medical care.  In the short
run, freeing up the medical market will drive down the costs of medicine
and thus the amount of spending needed to pay for our charity cases.

In the long run, Nick's right.  People will pay for medical treatment to
save their lives so long as there is any.

