From: Paul Rutkovsky <prutkov@mailer.cc.fsu.edu> 

SEE   JANE   RUN 
 
SHORT  SWEET  GONE 
 
TRANS  MISS   IONS 
 
 
FAX PHONE FIVE SECONDS 
 
A collaboration between the Department of Art, Creative Writing 
Program, and publisher Kevin Begos at Florida State University  
Gallery and Museum, Tallahassee, FL, December 9, 1992 
 
By Paul Rutkovsky 
 
An evening of events centered around the eventual reception of  
William Gibson's AGRIPPA (A Book of the Dead), programmed for 
computer viral self-destruction, was a lesson of sorts in 
telecommunications frustration. Organized by Gail Rubini, this  
event was in its planning stages for months and went through 
several revisions. The program at FSU stated: "Welcome to an  
evening of transmissions: both the chronolgy and the timing are  
mutable. Only the participants--and sometimes not even they--are  
responsible for the sequence of events."  The original objective  
was to transmit AGRIPPA to nine sites simultaneously around the  
world via a simple process---telephone line and modem/computer.  
Potentially this event would have been very process involved.   
Not knowing the exact time of the reception or the length of the  
text would have given the event a certain element of uncertain  
tension.  However, the last instructional revision was received 
two days before the "transmission."  Disks were sent to the sites  
stating that the text could not be sent successfully.  The original  
plan of telecommunicating spontaneously with text that would also  
self-destruct was not going to take place. 
 
I also participated on another level by organizing FAX PHONE FIVE  
SECONDS. Approximately thirty participants from around the U.S.A.,  
Canada, and Israel sent sound and fax works.  Between 7:00 and  
9:00 p.m. prose, poetry, and composed music works were received  
via live phone hooked up to a sound system in the gallery.  As the  
sound works played, faxes came in at specified times and were  
displayed in the gallery next to the phone and sound system.   
This process seemed to be far more engaging than what AGRIPPA had  
turned into.  Some people in the gallery assisted with putting 
faxes up on the wall, others rushed out to the local copy shop and  
faxed works to the gallery during the event and unexpectedly  
interrupted the fax time line.  There was an element of immediacy  
and excitement about faxes and sound works getting interrupted and  
sometimes bumped by zealous and "non-invited" last second  
participants.  It was an active and lively process event. 
 
AGRIPPA arrived on two disks with other paraphernalia crypticly  
related to the book.  On December 9th at approximately 8:00 p.m.  
in the gallery, Jerome Stern (Creative Writing Program) read the  
contents of AGRIPPA from the scrolling text on a Macintosh  
computer.  At the end of the story the text began to scroll  
backwards as it disintegrated---it was over.  The large audience  
stood silent for several seconds and then quietly applauded. 
 
