From: Wim van der Plas  <ISEA@RUGR86.RUG.NL> 
 
Subject: TISEA 
 
The Third International Symposium on Electronic Art took place in 
Sydney, from November 9-13. By symposium shuttle bus, we were 
transported from gallery to gallery (five in total) to see the 
TISEA art exhibitions. Some galleries had mainly Australian 
electronic art on display, others had only work from foreign 
artists. The general impression was very positive: electronic art 
is being taken seriously in Australia. 

We witnessed something of the workshops on Virtual Reality in the  
Museum of Contemporary Art (Monday and Tuesday)  and key note  
speaker Myron Krueger on Videoplace in the Powerhouse Museum and  
the Stelarc performance, this time together with an industrial  
robot arm geared with lights and video cameras. 

The next three days were devoted to the symposium in the prestigious 
Art Gallery of New South Wales and the adjacent Botanical Gardens. 
In the world famous Sydney Opera House was a daily showing  
of David Blair's 60 minutes video "WAX: or the discovery of  
television among the bees". Blair was present to answer questions  
about this remarkable science fiction art piece.
 
On Wednesday, the Electronic Theatre took place in the Art Gallery  
of NSW. John McCormack's remarkable TISEA animated logo 
was one of the highlights. On Thursday there was an 'Artists  
Reception' at the Performance Space Gallery. Here we saw a very  
interesting exhibition of interactive pieces. One of my  
favourites was "Light from Noise Sound" by Takuro Osaka from  
Japan. In a small room, one could witness changes of colours that  
were triggered by the changing sounds of the city outside of the  
gallery. 

Friday night was performance night, in the ABC Ultimo Centre,  
which is very well equipped for this kind of event. Very  
tasteful, although more mechanical than electronic, was Pierre  
Bastin's "Mecanium". A number of funny looking mechanical devices  
produce music, that Bastin accompanies by playing trumpet.  
Humourous and thought provoking was Yuji Sone's "Nonetheless  
Marinetti". He sat in front of a lighted, but empty screen, while  
his 'thoughts' could be read from a moving light display behind  
him. 

TISEA published an excellent catalogue/book of abstracts, partly  
in full colour. Negotiations are going on concerning the  
publication of Proceedings and a video tape. 
 
