Date: Mon, 1 Feb 93 10:16:40 -0500
From: Bill MacIntosh <wk01739@worldlink.com>
Subject: BOOK:  Rx 2000

Reply-To: Extropians@gnu.ai.mit.edu
Errors-To: Extropians-Request@gnu.ai.mit.edu

I recently read a book that may be of interest to this list.
It is called Rx 2000 by Dr. Jeffery A. Fisher.  It discusses
probable advances in medicine over the next 40 years.  The
style of the book is similar to Unbounding the Future.  Short
stories are told of medical procedures occuring to people in the
future. The basis of the story is then described, telling how 
the procedure or drug came about.

I found it interesting that many problems have a free radical 
involved somewhere in the chain of events.  Vitamin E, C and 
beta carotine are mentioned often as a soon to be generally
recognised method for combatting many problems.  There is
also a timeline in the middle of the book which gives a 
overview of progress.  The author is predicting a 250-300 yr.
life expectancy by 2030.

WARNING:  There are often glowing predictions of "socialize"
          medical practices and medical "big brother" activities.

I thought it was interesting that some of the described future
events that will occur (e.g. creating brainless fetuses to provide
tissue for various treatments) given the behavior of the FDA and 
other conservative forces.  I suppose it is not that unusual 
considering the author's belief that a medicine for all and a 
nation-wide electronic medical file system allowing specialists 
around the country to decide the treatment you will receive.

If you can read around the collectivist praises, the book has 
some interesting information on upcoming medical advances.

