Date: Mon, 1 Feb 93 09:25:23 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: ECON: Are Medicare Expenditures Too High?

A simple adjustment to health insurance policies and medicare would
restore consumer sensitivity to price.  Currently, with my medical
insurance at work, there is a $75 deductible per person, and I have
to pay 20% of the next $1500, and zero percent of the balance over
$1575.  This means each year I consider whether to have a general
checkup, but once I'm over the initial theshhold, the perceived
expense goes away.

Since the purpose of health insurance should be to spread the cost
of low incidence, high cost expenses, not to cover routine expenses,
the change would be to a more graduated co-payment schedule.

For example, I would have to pay 100% of the first $500 (what I
consider a reasonable insurance deductible), 30% of the next $2000,
20% of the next $7500, 10% of the next $20,000, and 5% thereafter.

The payments vs. annual health cost would run:

Health Expense		My payment

500			500
1000			800
2000			950
5000			1600
10000			2600
25000			4100
50000			5600 
100000			8100

Since the cost for major surgery can easily run to $100,000, I would
still have incentive to consider different cost alternatives.  A
change to a less expensive procedure might involve cutting the
cost in half, and I would see a $2500 diffrence in my out of pocket
cost.  The co-payments would be moderately painful, but not so
great as to break me financially.  This is the desired range,
so the consumer has incentive to select less expensive routes.

By moving the routine expenses into the deductible range, it would
also cut the paperwork overhead of the insurers ( you would only
submit a claim once your annual expenditure passes the initial
deductible), and the doctors.

Comments?

Dani
Eder


