Date: Mon, 1 Feb 1993 11:17 EST
From: KMOSTA01@ulkyvx02.louisville.edu
Subject: ECON: Are Medicare Expenditures Too High?

Dani Eder gives an alternative health insurance deductible and copayment
schedule which clearly is more rational than what prevails now. But ...
please explain how in the world any rational reform will be implemented
with the current administration and Congress. To me this is very
similar to arguing in Germany in 1943 that 14% of GDP on defence was
excessive and that it would have been better to find a negotiated
settlement with the West and cut military expenditures. Sure, it
would have been, and if you said that you would end up in Buchenwald.
In the U.S. no Buchenwald yet, but we have democracy and Dani's
solution, proposed many times over by economists and actuaries, will
be completely ignored. People who can think straight at least for a minute
can see what the pricing distortions are in the medical care area. The
question is not what the solution is, the question is -- how do we get
the political decision makers to implement them, and how do we get the
political forces to even notice market solutions (for those using
anti-market rhetoric, "market solutions" means solutions in which 
prices are not set by the government but by the buyers and sellers of
the good or service involved - we have very few of those left in the 
U.S. economy, if any). 
Krzys'

