Date: Mon, 1 Feb 1993 10:37:48 -0800
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: PPL: Many Types of Intellectual Property Are Not Protected

hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney) says:

>The problem with having no patents is that you can invent something, some
>major improvement in an industrial process, or a startling new gadget
>(like the zipper), or whatever.  But you can't profit from it because
>your competitors are able to copy what you've done.

Yes, the "problem" with having no state-enforced monopoly is that you have
to compete.  Figure out ways to take advantage of your particular talents 
and knowledge...or just get a job.

>Look at what happens in the drug business.  I am constantly reading that
>drug companies won't test various natural substances because they are not
>patentable, hence there is no opportunity to make profits to cover the
>expensive testing phase.

They are chasing the incentives of the current system.  Why work here 
when the carrot is dangling over there?

>(Granted, governments may make testing more expensive than necessary, but 
>I'm sure we all would prefer to buy well-tested drugs even under PPL.)

More like, everyone wants the drugs they take tested to *some* level, but
the cost of meeting regulations is beyond that.

>Will anyone here dispute that removing patent
>protection would slow the rate of new drug creation?

If you took away the carrot but kept the sticks?  

[Hal was responding to Tim, who...]
>...makes the good point that there are many areas where a lack of
>intellectual property protection has not stopped innovation...

But I think a more important point is: why should we social-engineer 
the rate (and kind, inevitably) of innovation using coercion?  People 
talking about grey goo say you can't supress invention.  But you can 
refrain from giving it artificial advantages over other activities.

-fnerd
